\details{
\code{method = "classical"} computes the Hausman (1978) statistic
\code{t(b_fe - b_re) \%*\% solve(V_fe - V_re) \%*\% (b_fe - b_re)} over the
coefficients common to both models (the random-effects intercept is
dropped), with a chi-square reference. It always uses the model-based
(classical) covariances, also when \code{fe} was fit with a robust \code{vcov}: the
contrast variance \code{V_fe - V_re} relies on the random-effects estimator
being efficient under the null, which holds only for model-based
covariances and independent, homoskedastic idiosyncratic errors.
In finite samples \code{V_fe - V_re} need not be positive definite and the
statistic can then be negative. The signed value is returned, with
\code{p.value = NA} and a warning (\code{plm::phtest()} reports its absolute value
instead); the regression-based \code{method = "mundlak"} has no such problem.

\code{method = "mundlak"} is the regression-based (correlated random effects)
version of the same test (Mundlak 1978; Wooldridge 2010, section 10.7.3),
valid under heteroskedasticity and serial correlation. The pooled model
\code{y ~ x + xbar} is fit with the individual means \code{xbar} of the time-varying
regressors of \code{fe}, and the null \code{coef(xbar) = 0} is tested with a Wald
statistic \code{W} using the \code{vcov} covariance. With \code{vcov = "cluster"} the
reported statistic is \code{F = W / k} with reference \code{F(k, G - 1)} (\code{G}
individuals), a small-sample correction for few clusters; otherwise it
is \code{W} with reference chi-square(k). Gaussian family only.

The Wald chi-square \code{W} and its chi-square p-value are always returned
(\code{wald}, \code{wald_p.value}) and printed. \code{W} is the statistic that the
summary of a \code{cre = TRUE} fit reports (\code{summary(fit)$cre_test}, a Wald
chi-square with the covariance stored on that fit): the two are equal for
\code{panglm(..., model = "pooling", family = "gaussian", cre = TRUE)} fit
with the same \code{vcov}, and close but not equal for a \code{model = "random"}
CRE fit, whose coefficients are GLS rather than pooled estimates. The
p-values differ only through the \code{F(k, G - 1)} reference used here with
\code{vcov = "cluster"}.
